## BMX.KEEP / BMX.RESTORE -- does the palette really go into spare VRAM
## and come back, and does it get there in ONE KERNAL call?
##
## BMX.PAINT writes the picture's colours over the machine's, and nothing
## on the X16 puts them back by itself -- neither SCREEN nor screen_mode
## restores a palette. So BMX.PAINT stashes all 256 entries first and
## BMX.RESTORE writes them back, both as a single memory_copy: VERA's two
## address ports aimed at the palette and at the stash, source $9F23 and
## target $9F24, neither stepped by the KERNAL because both are in
## $9F00-$9FFF. The KERNAL chapter names that use outright; this is the
## check that the machine agrees.
##
## FIVE THINGS, and the middle ones are the point:
##
##   A  the stash HOLDS the palette, byte for byte and IN ORDER. A source
##      that got stepped would read I/O space; a target VERA did not walk
##      would leave 512 copies of one byte. Comparing element by element
##      catches both, where a checksum might not.
##   B  THE SCRIBBLE TOOK. Every byte is overwritten with 255 minus
##      itself, which cannot equal the original for any byte value, so the
##      disturbed count must be 512 exactly. Without this, two copies that
##      both quietly did nothing would leave the before and after
##      palettes agreeing and the test would pass having proved nothing.
##   C  the restore is byte exact, all 512.
##   D  a second BMX.RESTORE is harmless -- a caller that does not know
##      whether its picture ever loaded must be able to call it anyway.
##   E  the REAL path: a genuine picture through BMX.SHOW, which calls
##      BMX.KEEP itself, then one BMX.RESTORE. A..D drive the two halves
##      by hand; this drives nothing but a filename, which is the whole
##      claim being made for the interface.
##
## Measured 2026-08-19: PASS on all five. The stash took 512 of 512 in
## order, the scribble disturbed 512, the restore was exact, and painting
## BEARDGUY.BMX moved a majority of the palette which one call put back.
#INCLUDE "GPB.INC.BL"
#INCLUDE "BMX.INC.BL"
#SAVEAS "@:BMXPAL.PRG"

#DEFINE SCRMODE $FF5F

## The whole palette: 256 entries of 2 bytes at VRAM $1:FA00.
#DEFINE PALBYTES 512
#DEFINE PALBASE $FA00

## BMX.STASH's default, $13000, as the offset VPEEK wants in bank 1.
#DEFINE STASHOFF $3000

DIM WAS%(PALBYTES), GOT%(PALBYTES)
NDX = 0 : BAD = 0 : HIT = 0 : PICHIT = 0 : PICBAD = 0

## ---- A. the palette as the ROM left it, then keep it

FOR NDX = 0 TO PALBYTES - 1
    WAS%(NDX) = VPEEK(1, PALBASE + NDX)
NEXT

TK = TI
GOSUB BMX.KEEP
TKK = TI - TK
EK$ = BMX.ERROR$

BAD = 0
FOR NDX = 0 TO PALBYTES - 1
    GOT%(NDX) = VPEEK(1, STASHOFF + NDX)
    IF GOT%(NDX) <> WAS%(NDX) THEN BAD = BAD + 1
NEXT
STASHBAD = BAD

## ---- B. scribble, and prove the scribble landed

FOR NDX = 0 TO PALBYTES - 1
    VPOKE 1, PALBASE + NDX, 255 - WAS%(NDX)
NEXT

HIT = 0
FOR NDX = 0 TO PALBYTES - 1
    IF VPEEK(1, PALBASE + NDX) <> WAS%(NDX) THEN HIT = HIT + 1
NEXT

## ---- C. put it back

TR = TI
GOSUB BMX.RESTORE
TRR = TI - TR

BAD = 0
FOR NDX = 0 TO PALBYTES - 1
    IF VPEEK(1, PALBASE + NDX) <> WAS%(NDX) THEN BAD = BAD + 1
NEXT
PUTBAD = BAD

## ---- D. a second restore must do nothing at all

GOSUB BMX.RESTORE
BAD = 0
FOR NDX = 0 TO PALBYTES - 1
    IF VPEEK(1, PALBASE + NDX) <> WAS%(NDX) THEN BAD = BAD + 1
NEXT
TWICEBAD = BAD

## ---- E. the real path, through BMX.SHOW

GP.CALL SCRMODE, 128, 0, 0, 0
BMX.FILE$ = "BEARDGUY.BMX"
GOSUB BMX.SHOW
EP$ = BMX.ERROR$

PICHIT = 0
FOR NDX = 0 TO PALBYTES - 1
    IF VPEEK(1, PALBASE + NDX) <> WAS%(NDX) THEN PICHIT = PICHIT + 1
NEXT

GOSUB BMX.RESTORE
PICBAD = 0
FOR NDX = 0 TO PALBYTES - 1
    IF VPEEK(1, PALBASE + NDX) <> WAS%(NDX) THEN PICBAD = PICBAD + 1
NEXT

GP.CALL SCRMODE, 0, 0, 0, 0
PRINT "A STASH WRONG ";STASHBAD;" EXPECT 0   [";EK$;"]"
PRINT "B SCRIBBLE HIT";HIT;" EXPECT";PALBYTES
PRINT "C RESTORE BAD ";PUTBAD;" EXPECT 0"
PRINT "D TWICE BAD   ";TWICEBAD;" EXPECT 0"
PRINT "E PICTURE MOVED";PICHIT;" EXPECT >0  [";EP$;"]"
PRINT "  AFTER RESTORE";PICBAD;" EXPECT 0"
PRINT "  KEEP";TKK;" JIFFIES  RESTORE";TRR;" JIFFIES"
PRINT "@DONE@"
POWEROFF
